readings.c2s1:
{{smaller block|He stopped his little nag short of the crest, and got off and looked ahead of him from behind a ''tump'' of whortles.
{{right|''R. D. Blackmore'', Lorna Doone, xxxi.}}}}

'''tump''' (tump), ''v. t.'' [< ''tump'', ''n.''] In ''hort.'', to form a mass of earth or a hillock round (a plant): as, to ''tump'' teazel.

'''tump-line''' (tump′līn), ''n.'' [Perhaps a corruption, among the Canadian Indians and the French voyageurs, of E. *''temple-line'' (or of a corresponding F. term), < ''temple''<sup>2</sup> (F. ''tempe'') + ''line''<sup>2</sup>.] A strap by which a pack is carried across a portage or through the woods. {{smaller|It crosses the forehead, the advantage being that its use in this position leaves the hands free for clearing the way with an ax or otherwise; it is frequently shifted in position so as to cross the breast, for temporary relief. This method of carrying is common through the St. Lawrence valley and to the furthest Northwest, alike among whites, half-breeds, and Indians. The term is used in Maine and on its borders: elsewhere the strap is called ''portage-strap'' or ''pack-strap''.}}

'''tumpy''' (tum′pi), ''a.'' [< ''tump'' + ''-y''<sup>1</sup>.] Abounding in tumps or hillocks; uneven. ''Halliwell.'' [Prov. Eng.]

'''tum-tum''' (tum′tum), ''n.'' [Appar. ult. imitative of the beating of a drum; cf. ''tum''<sup>2</sup> and ''tom-tom''.] '''1.''' A favorite dish in the West Indies, made by beating boiled plantains quite soft in a wooden mortar. It is eaten like a potato-pudding, or made into round cakes and fried.—'''2.''' Same as ''tom-tom''.

readings.c2s3:
¶
{{smaller block|What meaneth the noise of this ''tumult''? 1 Sam. iv. 14.}}
{{smaller block|There is this difference between the ''tumults'' here [in Cairo] and those at Constantinople, that the latter are commonly begun by some resolute fellows among the janizaries, whereas here the mob is generally raised by some great man, who envies one that is a rival to him.
{{right|''Pococke'', Description of the East, I. 169.}}}}
'''2.''' Violent commotion or agitation, with confusion of sounds.
{{smaller block|In this piece of poetry, what can be nobler than the idea it gives us of the Supreme Being thus raising a ''tumult'' among the elements, and recovering them out of their confusion, thus troubling and becalming nature?
{{right|''Addison'', Spectator, No. 489.}}}}
'''3.''' Agitation; high excitement; irregular or confused motion.
{{smaller block|The ''tumult'' in her mind seemed not yet abated.
{{right|''Goldsmith'', Vicar, xix.}}}}
{{smaller|='''Syn.''' Disturbance, turbulence, confusion, hubbub, ferment, outbreak, mêlée.}}

'''tumult†''' (tū′mult), ''v. i.'' [< ''tumult'', ''n.'' Cf. ''tumultuate.''] To make a tumult; be in great commotion. ''Milton'', Hist. Eng., vi.

'''tumulter†''' (tū′mul-tėr), ''n.'' [< ''tumult'' + ''-er''<sup>1</sup>.] One who raises or takes part in a tumult. ''Hakluyt’s Voyages'', I. 466.

'''tumultuarily''' (tū-mul′t̮ū-ā-ri-li), ''adv.'' '''1.''' In a tumultuary or disorderly manner.
{{smaller block|Divers thousands of the Jews ''tumultuarily'' resisted.
{{right|''Sandys'', Christ’s Passion (1640), notes, p. 95.}}}}
'''2.''' Without system or order.
{{smaller block|I have, according to your desire, putt in writing these Minutes of Lives ''tumultuarily'', as they occur’d to my thoughts, or as occasionally I had information of them.}}

readings.c3s3:
¶
'''tumultus''' (tū-mul′tus), ''n.'' [L., commotion, tumult: see ''tumult.''] Commotion; irregular action.—'''Tumultus cordis''', {{smaller|irregular action of the heart.}}—'''Tumultus sermonis''', {{smaller|a form of aphasia in which the patient stutters when reading aloud.}}

'''tumulus''' (tū′mū-lus), ''n.''; pl. ''tumuli'' (-lī). [< L. ''tumulus'', a mound, < ''tumere'', swell: see ''tumid.'' Cf. ''tump''<sup>1</sup> and ''tomb.''] A sepulchral mound, as the famous Mound of Marathon raised over the bodies of those Athenians who fell in repelling the invading Persians; a barrow; very frequently, a mound covering and inclosing a more or less elaborate structure of masonry.
{{smaller block|The raising of mounds over the tombs of the dead, particularly of distinguished persons, or those slain in battle, was a usual practice among very many peoples from the most remote antiquity.}}

'''tun'''<sup>1</sup> (tun), ''n.'' [Also ''ton'' (now used only in the sense of a measure); early mod. E. ''tunne, tonne,'' < ME. ''tunne, tonne,'' < AS. ''tunne'' = MD. ''tonne,'' D. ''ton'' = OHG. ''tunna,'' MHG. ''tunne,'' G. ''tonne'' = Icel. ''tunna'' = Sw. ''tunna,'' OSw. ''tynna'' = Dan. ''tönde;'' cf. F. ''tonne'' (dim. ''tonneau,'' OF. ''tonnel'' = Pr. Sp. Pg. dim. ''tonel''), ML. ''tunna,'' Ir. and Gael. ''tunna;'' root unknown; it is uncertain whether the Teut. or the Celtic forms are original. Hence ''tunnel.''] '''1.''' A large cask for holding liquids, especially wine, ale, or beer. See ''ton''<sup>1</sup>.
{{smaller block|As who so filled a ''tonne'' of a fresshe ryuer,<br>And went forth with that water to woke with [add water to] Themese. {{right|''Piers Plowman'' (B), xv. 331.}}
<poem>Take four and twenty bucks and ewes,
And ten ''tun'' of the wine.</poem>
{{right|''Childe Vyet'' (Child’s Ballads, II. 75).}}}}
